WEBVTT

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.490
JUSTINE COCCHI: Hi,
I'm Justine Cocchi,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.940
and I'm a Program Manager on
the Azure Cosmos DB team.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.340
I'm joined today
by Aayush Kataria,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.640
who's a software engineer
also working on Cosmos DB.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.260
We're excited to
talk to you today

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.170
about designing reliable
multi-agent apps

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.990
with Azure Cosmos
DB, and the example

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.540
that we'll use today
is a travel assistant,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.180
which is a multi-agent
AI application

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.970
that helps you plan
personalized trips.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.170
One of the functionalities

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.740
of our application
is a chat feature.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.710
When a user wants to plan a
trip, they can start a chat

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.590
by saying something like
"Plan a trip to Paris."

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.590
This first gets routed to
our orchestrator agent,

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.150
who then can pass the message

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.600
to various specialist agents,
like the hotel agent.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.260
We can look up memories in
Azure Cosmos DB with contacts

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.870
that we already have
stored about this user.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.440
We can embed the initial
query with Azure Open AI,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.080
so that we can turn it into
vectors that we can then search

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.230
against our existing other
metadata, like places

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.840
and restaurants and
hotels in Paris

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.350
that may be useful for our user.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.590
Then we're ready to send our
message back with the data

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:15.240
that we found and help
them plan their trip.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.240
This is just an example of
one turn, and if we look

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.080
at the user interaction flow,
we can see many pieces of data

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.860
that are moving through this
user journey, from the message

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.800
to the various agents we're
talking to, when we're ready

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.200
to actually create
the trip, and lastly,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.640
we need to extract the memories

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.880
so that we can remember what's
happened next time the user

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.430
comes and logs into our website.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.500
There's various things
that we want to focus

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.080
on in different parts
of the user flow

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.080
that will really help us keep
the best user experience.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.610
In the beginning, the latency

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.220
and personalization is
the most important.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.680
We want to make sure the
agent remembers key things

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.280
about our users and that the
responses are very fast.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.830
Otherwise, the user may become
frustrated and they may go

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.530
to one of our competitor
travel sites.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.540
Whereas, in the back
half, consistency

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.580
and reliability become
more important.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.890
We don't want to accidentally
double-book a trip

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.530
or forget something that
we already talked about.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.740
So really keeping in mind
the user perspective

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.560
as we build our application
will help us ensure

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.930
that our application
is reliable.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.610
Now, I want to zoom
in on one thing,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.440
which is the number
of agents here.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.760
We've talked about
an orchestrator

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.140
and some specialists.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.510
I want to hand it off to Aayush
who's going to talk more

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.950
about the multi-agent pattern

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.680
and how it can help us
in our application.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.820
AAYUSH KATARIA: Thanks, Justine.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.470
Going over the travel scenario
that we'll be discussing today.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.210
Hi, I'm Aayush Kataria.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.510
I'm a software engineer
at Azure Cosmos DB.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.870
So let's dive into
why multi-agent.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.620
So let's start with what
goes wrong when you don't.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.460
So on the left, you see
the single-agent problem.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.470
When you have one agent trying

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.170
to handle every
domain all packed

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.500
into a single system prompt,
the model gets confused.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.400
It doesn't know which
capability to invoke or when.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.560
There's no separation
of concerns,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.980
so every change risks
breaking something else.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.640
You can't scale or improve
any area of expertise

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.530
without touching everything.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.570
And as conversations
grow longer,

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.280
you hit context window limits.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.710
The model starts
forgetting earlier parts

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.190
of the conversation, leading
to inconsistent behavior.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.210
So on the right-hand side, we
have the multi-agent solution.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.110
Instead of one monolithic agent,
you have specialized agents,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:38.100
each with a focus prompt and
a specific set of tools.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.450
So an orchestrator sits on
top, routes the user's request

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.360
to the right specialist
based on the intent,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.670
so this gives us a few big wins.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.850
So first, each agent
has a smaller,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.360
more focused context,
so it reasons better.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.650
Second, you can develop and
update agents independently.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.150
Adding a new specialist doesn't
require touching existing ones.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.220
Third, it's composable.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.770
You can plug in new capabilities

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.010
without rewriting
the whole system.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:16.380
So now let's look at how
agentic memory works better

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.340
than retrieval-augmented
generation.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.420
So this is where we
get into, I think,

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.240
which is the most interesting
part of the stock.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.010
That is agentic memory.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.250
So most people are
familiar with RAG.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.980
That's retrieval-augmented
generation.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.530
You have a knowledge base,
you embed documents,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.880
and when a user asks a question,
you retrieve the relevant chunks

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.560
and feed them to the model.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.950
So it works well
for what it does

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.960
but has fundamental limitations

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.840
when you're building
agent-based applications.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.970
So let's walk through
the comparison.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.260
Traditional RAG is static.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.070
Your knowledge base is fixed
until you explicitly update it.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.620
Agentic memory, on the
other hand, is dynamic.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.460
It grows automatically
with every interaction.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.040
RAG gives the same
results to everyone.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.500
It's searching a shared corpus.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.960
Agentic memory is user specific.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.350
It stores individual
preferences and history,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.680
so two different
users get completely

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.240
different experiences.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.720
RAG retrieves by
similarity alone.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.870
Whatever is closest in the
embedding space comes back.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.960
Agentic memory uses salient
scoring, which factors

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.230
in importance, confidence,
and resiliency.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.570
RAG doesn't learn
from interactions.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.270
It's just a pure lookup.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.630
Agentic memory actively
learns and adapts.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.270
Every conversation
is an opportunity

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.060
to extract new knowledge.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.240
And RAG has no concept of time.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.360
Agentic memory supports
cross-session persistence

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.550
with TTL policies.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.330
Let's now go over the
system architecture

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.830
for our travel agent application
that we are going to demo

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.140
at the later end of the session.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:12.690
So now let's zoom out and look
at the full system architecture.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.660
So everything starts
when a message lands

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.520
in the orchestrator agent.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.100
This is the front door
of our application,

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:23.730
so every conversation comes
through the orchestrator first.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:28.500
Its only job is to
understand intent and decide

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.290
which specialist
should handle it.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.050
After that, there are
specialized agents --

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.690
hotel, dining, and activity.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:42.330
Each one owns a single domain,
so has its own system prompt

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.760
and pulls from a
focused set of tools.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.840
In our multi-agent application,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.430
none of these agents are talking
directly to the database.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:58.110
They go through the MCP
server, which has many tools,

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:01.050
like recall memories,
discover places,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.830
transfer between different
agents, and many more.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:10.920
The point of MCP is decoupling,
so we can update or add tools

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.030
without touching agent code,
and one tool can be reused

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.910
across every other agent.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.900
So underneath the MCP layer,
we have our Azure Cosmos DB.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.290
It stores everything the
application needs, sessions,

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:30.390
messages, places, trips,
API events, among others.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.750
Places are searched
using hybrid search,

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:37.650
and every container uses a
hierarchical partition key,

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:41.430
so the system is
multi-tenant from day one.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.190
Next, we have the
agent memory toolkit,

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.100
which is a new
Cosmos DB offering

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.380
to host your memory layer, so
for any multi-agent application.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.410
It is a separate
background runtime

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.320
that hands off the
same Cosmos DB account

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.850
that I mentioned before.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:03.540
It owns its own container
called "memories"

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.230
where the actual turns of the
conversation, facts extracted

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.170
from those turns, the user
summaries, the thread summaries,

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:15.270
everything is stored, and the
agent never calls it directly.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.270
And then we have
the Azure OpenAI

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.520
which is providing
the LLM reasoning

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.410
and embedding capabilities.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:26.610
It's a shared utility that
feeds both the agent runtime

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.040
and the toolkit pipeline.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:35.340
Now let's go over some concepts
about memory and agentic memory.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.490
So before we go
deeper into memory,

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.520
let's establish a
clear vocabulary.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.780
People use the word "memory"
loosely, so let's be precise.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.500
So there are three
distinct concepts here.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.290
State, which is the snapshot

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:55.160
of your entire application
at any given moment.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.940
It contains everything needed
to restore the application

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:01.910
to that exact point,
what the user said,

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:05.040
what the agent decided,
what tools were called.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.270
Then we have short-term
memory, which is what happens

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:12.450
within that particular
single session.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.280
It's the conversation history,

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.740
the messages going
back and forth.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.590
This is what lets
an agent refer back

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.650
to something the user
said three messages ago

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.290
and give a coherent response.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.240
Then we have the
long-term memory.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:31.350
This is where things actually
start to get more powerful.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.420
This persists across sessions.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.930
When a user comes back
days or weeks later,

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:40.380
the agent can recall what it
learned about them previously,

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.740
their preferences, past
decisions, feedback,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.650
and this is what
enables personalization

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:48.680
and learning over time.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.810
It's the difference
between an agent

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:53.720
that starts fresh every
conversation and one

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:57.270
that builds a relationship
with the user.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.470
JUSTINE COCCHI: So Aayush,

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.920
these are really interesting
different types of memory.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:03.870
I'm wondering, how can
we implement these

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.090
into our travel application?

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:06.630
AAYUSH KATARIA: Yeah.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.110
So in our travel application, we
are going to implement all three

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.240
of these different
kind of memories.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.750
State management through
LangGraph Cosmos DB checkpointer

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.140
integration, and then short-term
memory and long-term memory

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.590
through the dedicated agent
memory toolkit backed

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.330
by Cosmos DB.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:27.420
Now let's move over to
the code and I'll go

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:31.080
over a few more core concepts,
and then we can jump

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.560
into our UI demo and look

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.530
and get the feel how the
agent memory toolkit works

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.020
with our multi-agent
application.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.040
So we just talked

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.070
about long-term memory
at a high level.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.140
Now I want to break that down

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.500
because not all long-term
memories are the same,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.360
and the toolkit reflects

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.510
that with four different
types of memories.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:58.650
So the reason this matters
is each type has a different

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:01.830
lifetime, a different
way of being created,

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.530
and a different way
the agent uses it.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.270
So let's walk through them.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:11.790
As you can see here, we have a
few different types of memory

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:15.030
that we are storing in
our agent memory toolkit.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:19.440
First is the facts, so sometimes
called "semantic memory."

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.010
These are simple, durable
statements about the user.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:27.090
User is vegetarian, travels with
a wheelchair, lives in Seattle.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:28.530
They are objectively true.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.920
They don't change often and
they should never expire.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.470
Second is the procedural memory.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.160
This is behavioral patterns,
preferences inferred

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.440
from how the user
actually behaves

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:44.820
over multiple conversations,
tends to book boutique hotels

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.760
over chains, prefers
late dinners.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.170
These are softer than facts, so
the user might never say them

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:55.650
out loud, but they
emerge from repetition.

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:58.860
They also never
expire by default

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:02.670
because behavioral patterns
are usually stable.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:05.310
Third is episodic memory.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:09.480
These are tied to a
specific time and place.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.000
Visited New York
City in last spring,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.940
loved seafood on
my Barcelona trip.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:19.110
These are how the system knows
to reference a past trip

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:24.000
when planning a new one,
and these do expire.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.460
So the default is 90 days.

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:30.780
Episodic memory gets a
little noisy too fast,

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:34.560
and a recommendation
system doesn't really need

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.060
to remember a coffee shop

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:39.390
that you went probably
four years ago.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.790
Fourth is the user summary.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:44.070
This is different
from the other three.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.400
It's not extracted
from a single message.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.150
It's a rolling synthesis
of who this user I,

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:54.360
regenerated automatically
every few turns.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.390
It can be something like

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.290
"This user is a frequent
solo traveler, vegetarian,

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:06.660
prefers cultural over
outdoor, mid-range hotels."

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:10.560
So the orchestrator pulls
these into its system prompt

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.040
at the start of
every conversation.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.610
So even on day one of
a brand-new session,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.780
the agent already
knows the user.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:27.460
So let's go and look
how it looks in the UI.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.620
So let me sign in
with a user, Tony,

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:38.040
for which we already have
some preferences loaded.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.320
So let me go talk with
the chat assistant.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:49.690
Let's say, "Hi, I am
planning a trip to Tokyo,"

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:55.230
and you'd be able to see that
it's actually in the logs

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.870
where my API server is running

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.060
that it's actually going
to the orchestrator.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.220
Orchestrator then
works its magic,

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:05.550
figure out what's the intent
of the particular user,

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:10.290
what tools would I be
using, where should I go,

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.770
and then eventually it gives
us a response that, "Hello,

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.570
to get started with
Tokyo trip planning,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:19.350
please let me know
what hotels you want,

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:21.210
what activities you want to do."

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:22.050
So let's see.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:30.060
Let's ask it what are
my hotel preferences,

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:34.380
and if we'll actually head back
to the portal, we can actually,

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:37.110
for that particular user
in our memories container,

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:41.670
I can search what all
facts are actually stored.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:48.660
So if I'll execute this query, I
can see the user's name is Tony.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.750
Tony prefers luxury
five-star hotels.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:53.550
He loves art museums

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:56.700
and contemporary
galleries and everything.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.110
So if we'll head back to our
UI, I can actually see that,

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.660
based on your past interactions,
here's what I know

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.460
about your hotel preferences.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:09.630
You prioritize reliable Wi-Fi
over price for business trips.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:13.710
You enjoy rooftop bars and quiet
evenings during work travel.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:18.240
So similarly, I would be
able to fetch my preferences

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.270
for activities, my
preferences for restaurants,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:26.400
my dietary preferences.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.240
So that way the agent toolkit
is extracting the memories,

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.330
storing those preferences
back in Cosmos DB,

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:37.170
and when the user is coming,
and actually chatting

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.160
with the chat assistant.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.910
It's able to leverage those
preferences and memories back

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.950
into the conversation.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.940
Now let's head back
to the code once more

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:53.250
and let's see how are we
actually extracting all

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:54.420
these memories.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:58.530
So storing memories is
only half the challenge.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:03.300
The real value comes from making
all this memory intelligent,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:07.920
meaning the system actively
manages its own knowledge.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:12.330
So if we'll go to this
particular class,

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:17.730
we have public-facing APIs
which are extracting memories,

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:20.610
generating threat summary,
generating user summary,

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:24.060
and reconciling memories, which
means conflict resolution.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:27.240
So the first piece is the
threshold-driven extraction,

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.490
which is extract memories.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:33.900
So the real value comes from
making memories intelligent.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:37.830
So the user never has to
say, "Remember this."

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.870
As the conversation unfolds,

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:43.470
a background pipeline
runs every end turn

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:47.070
in the agent memory
toolkit and asks the LLM,

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:50.490
"Is there anything new about
this user worth remembering?"

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:54.930
If the user is -- if the answer
is yes, it pulls out a fact,

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:58.800
classifies it as a fact,
procedural or episodic memory,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.560
and writes it back to Cosmos DB.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.020
The agent loop
doesn't wait on this.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:07.500
It happens asynchronously
in the background.

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:11.280
The second piece is conflict
resolution, which is done

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:15.300
by the reconciled
memories method.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:19.740
So real conversations always
would be contradicting.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.320
The user says "I'm
vegan" on Monday.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:25.800
Three weeks later, they mention
how much they love a steakhouse

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.710
and they have
started eating meat.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.830
So now what does the system do?

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:32.360
So on a separate cadence,

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:36.230
the toolkit periodically
sweeps the user's facts

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:41.120
and asks the LLM to classify
the pairs of related memories

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:44.610
so they can be either
duplicates or updates.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.970
When it finds one, it
doesn't delete the user.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:51.330
It marks it superseded
by the winner.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:54.510
That is done so that the
audit trail is preserved,

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:57.510
so the user never
sees a contradiction

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.010
in the recommendations.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.130
But if you're debugging,
say, six months later,

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.860
you can still see exactly when

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.490
and why the system
changed its mind.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:11.940
The third place is
rolling summaries.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:13.920
Conversations can get long.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.140
Token budgets are finite.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.230
The toolkit handles this
by generating two kinds

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.030
of summaries on a cadence.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.920
So one is threat summary

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.990
that captures the
current conversation,

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.820
the current session,
and the thread,

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:31.290
and other is user summary that
captures who this user is

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.830
across all conversations.

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:38.930
So just for this demo
purposes, both user summary

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:40.880
and threat summary
are regenerated

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.930
after every five turns, so we
can actually see that happen

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:47.490
in the demo in a few minutes.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:53.760
In production, the agent toolkit
has like some default set

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.540
which are tunable by the
customer who's going

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.150
to use the agent memory toolkit.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:03.630
So the orchestrator eventually
pulls these summaries

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:07.260
into context at the
start of every turn,

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.600
so even after a
hundred messages,

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:13.950
the agent doesn't
lose the actual plot.

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:18.060
The fourth piece is what
I think of as the payoff.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:20.880
That is memory condition search.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:25.890
So everything that we've talked
about so far is plumbing.

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:30.410
This is where the memory shapes
what the user actually sees.

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:34.760
So before any specialist
agent searches for a hotel

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:38.030
or a restaurant, it
recalls memories first

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:40.520
to ask the toolkit,
"What do we know

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:44.550
about this user that's relevant
to this particular query?"

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:48.480
So the recall comes back with
relevant facts and preferences,

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.150
and the agent then encodes those

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:54.360
as explicit filters
on the search call.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:20:00.060
That's how "Show me
hotels in Tokyo" becomes

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.730
"Show me luxury
hotels in Tokyo."

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:07.140
So the personalization
isn't bolted on at the end.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.530
It's baked into
the query itself.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:14.790
So the agent memory toolkit
runs all these automatically

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.790
in the background on
the right cadence

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:21.420
without the application code
having to coordinate them,

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:24.240
and your agent
always stays simple.

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.940
So now let's go back to the UI

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:30.960
and let's see how we'll
choose a new user

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.830
that has no preferences stored
in our memories container.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:39.900
So let's see how
the workflow is.

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:41.790
So let me close this.

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:44.680
Let me log out of this session.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:49.560
Let's choose a new
user as Peter,

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:51.750
and we can go in our portal.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.340
We can confirm that Peter
doesn't have anything stored

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:59.350
in the memories container.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.030
Let's go back here.

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.950
Let's start a session.

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:12.960
Let's say I'm planning
a trip to Paris.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.950
Similarly, again, now if I'll
go to my API server back

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:19.960
in the terminal
where it's running,

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:25.890
I can see now it's the user
is the user chosen as Peter.

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:28.680
It's going to the
orchestrator agent and, like,

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:31.260
other tools that the
orchestrator agent decides

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:32.640
to go to.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.290
Let's head back to our UI.

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:35.700
I have an answer.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:39.090
So, "Hello, I'd be happy to help
you plan your trip to Paris.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:41.610
Would you like to start by
finding hotels, restaurants,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:44.790
or activities, or perhaps
