00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:04.890
You can use the vector store
integration from LangChain.

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.920
If you want to do
state checkpointing

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:09.390
for your multi-agent
application,

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:12.030
you can use the LangGraph
checkpointer store.

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:13.050
JUSTINE COCCHI: So Aayush,

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:16.500
it looks like we've
implemented the memory toolkit

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:20.700
and LangChain, and there's a lot
of touch points with Cosmos DB

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:22.470
and many different containers.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:24.240
How can we verify that
we're following all

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:27.480
of the Cosmos DB best
practices in this application?

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:29.730
AAYUSH KATARIA: That's a
really good question, Justine.

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:32.370
So let me set up the scene.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:37.350
So most of the AI apps I've
been building lately work.

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:38.790
They pass the demos.

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:41.850
They look great in a
notebook, but there's a gap

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:44.670
between it runs and
it runs at scale

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:47.670
without setting money on fire.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:52.320
So in Cosmos DB that gap
is paved with decisions

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:56.370
that you make on day one
and pay for on day 90,

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:59.580
that the partition keys you
didn't think hard enough about,

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:03.540
the indexing policy you left on
defaults, the document shape

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:07.470
that looked clean in code
but blows up your IU build

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:12.720
in production, so that's why
we are introducing a Cosmos DB

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:14.610
agent kit.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.550
So I'll show you.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.880
So you just need this
one line of code

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:24.600
that can just install
