And what these skills do is
they give the agent Cosmos DB

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:29.250
instincts of someone
who has shipped,

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:32.550
who has been shipping
on it for years.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:35.970
So partitioning,
indexing, RU economics,

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:39.300
modeling for actual
access patterns instead

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:42.690
of how the data
feels in your head.

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:48.480
So here's the report ranked
by impact, highest ROI first.

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.400
So let's just go
over the report.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:55.830
So it's giving you some
critical recommendations.

00:30:55.830 --> 00:31:00.120
It's giving you some
really highly important

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:04.170
recommendations, and you can
also interact with the agent

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:06.930
if you want to push
back on something

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:09.450
and you can tell the agent

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:13.020
that why did you did
something in a certain way.

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:17.670
So it can be an interactive
collaborative effort with that,

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:19.590
with this particular agent.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:21.660
So who is this for?

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:25.440
So if you're new to Cosmos
DB, the cognitive tax

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:29.490
of am I modeling this
right basically goes away.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:32.280
So you write code, the
agent can reviews it,

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:37.290
and you ship something that's
well architected by default.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:40.980
So if you -- and if you're a
seasoned Cosmos DB developer,

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:43.920
point it at your existing
services and let it hunt

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:47.100
for the things that have
quietly been costing you,

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.610
so like old containers
with stale index policies,

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:54.240
queries that grew teeth
over time, partition keys

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.460
that stopped aging well.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:32:01.530
And the part that genuinely
changes how teams operate is you

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:04.920
can drop this into your
CI and your PR reviews.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:09.930
Now every change gets a Cosmos
DB expert opinion before it

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:14.220
merges, so automatically
every time at no extra cost.

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:18.900
So that's the leverage of
using the Cosmos DB agent kit.

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:23.280
This Cosmos DB agent kit is
generally available now.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:23.820
JUSTINE COCCHI: Awesome.

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:24.720
Thanks, Aayush.

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.600
Now we can feel confident that
we have all the best practices

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:33.360
in place for partitioning and
cost management, but I also want

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:35.040
to make sure we're
production-ready

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.530
and that we're using
all the best practices

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.140
for reliability
and availability.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:43.650
And really, before we analyze
how to do that, it's important

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:46.830
to go back to the user
requirements and what we need

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:50.550
to guarantee to our users in
terms of three key metrics.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:54.270
The first metric is SLO,
which is the target level

00:32:54.270 --> 00:32:55.860
of service availability.

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:59.130
Now, we have a global
application and we want

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:01.410
to make sure that our
application is always available

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:05.250
for our users that may be
chatting from all over the world

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:08.310
at any time of day, so we
really can't have any downtime

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.110
in our application.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:13.290
And we also want to look
at two metrics for RPO,

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:17.640
recovery point objective, and
RTO, recovery time objective.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:21.030
Now, for distributed cloud
applications, it's not a matter

00:33:21.030 --> 00:33:24.330
of if a disaster will strike,
but when, and to ensure

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:26.910
that we're prepared
when something happens.

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:28.980
So for recovery point objective,

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:32.940
we need to define how long
before the disaster strikes

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:35.670
could we potentially
lose data and ensure

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:39.360
that our application
has RPO targets

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.220
that meet our user needs.

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:45.360
Now, after the disaster, there's
a potential time of downtime.

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:49.260
So how long is it acceptable
for us to be down?

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:52.530
In our application, we really
want five-nines of availability.

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:55.110
We want our application to
be always up and we want it

00:33:55.110 --> 00:33:58.050
to be available and
all around the world.

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:01.380
So we can use the active-active
pattern in Azure Cosmos DB

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:03.300
to ensure that we're
getting those five-nines

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:04.770
of availability.

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:06.930
Now, in Cosmos DB, there's
two different ways

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:09.060
to achieve the
active-active pattern.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:11.220
The first is with
the multi-writer.

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:14.790
In the multi-writer, you have
multiple Azure Cosmos DB regions

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:17.400
and they're all simultaneously
accepting both read

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:18.900
and write traffic.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:22.290
This is leaderless, meaning
writes can come into any region

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:25.140
and their conflict resolved
across all regions.

00:34:25.140 --> 00:34:27.870
In this setup, the RTO is zero

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:30.090
because all regions
are already online

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:31.860
and ready to accept traffic.

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:34.590
The RPO is effectively zero.

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:36.420
It's basically the
conflict resolution

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:39.270
and the replication
time across regions.

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:42.240
Now, in the single-writer
setup, this is enabled

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:45.780
by a new feature called "per
partition automatic failover."

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:49.890
This allows us to fail over at
the individual partition level.

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:51.840
Rather than waiting
for an entire region

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:55.470
to become unavailable and
failover after that happens,

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:59.130
we're able to react in a much
more agile way by responding

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.050
at the partition level.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.440
This is when you think about
a brownout versus a blackout.

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:06.930
It's much less common for
an entire region to go

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:08.790
down than an
individual partition

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:10.740
to maybe experience failures

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:13.410
that require failing
over to a new region.

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:15.900
With per partition
automatic failover,

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:20.520
the unaffected healthy regions,
or partitions, are still reading

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:22.800
and writing from that
primary write region,

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:26.040
where any partition that is
experiencing issues can fail

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:27.600
over seamlessly.

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.970
The application handles all
of this, so we don't need

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:33.000
to coordinate the failures in
our app side, and we don't need

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.010
to make an expensive
call on when

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:36.990
to failover the entire region.

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:39.180
So this gives us the
active-active pattern

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:41.070
with five-nines of availability

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:44.370
without actually having
multiple writers.

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:47.700
Per partition automatic failover
is now generally available.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:50.580
This gives us zero
downtime, zero data loss,

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:53.400
and with zero touch because
the SDK does the heavy lifting

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:54.840
for us.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:58.700
So now we know that
we have availability

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:01.310
at the partition level,
but we still do need

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:03.950
to manage some
complex operations

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:07.010
in our application
on the write path.

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:09.290
Even though our rights are
guaranteed to be available

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:11.270
because Cosmos DB will be up,

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:14.190
we need to make sure we
still have consistency.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:17.490
Imagine the scenario our
user has planned the trip

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.190
with our travel app and
they're ready to book.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:21.930
So when they say "Book it,"

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:24.660
three things are really
happening independently.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:27.690
First, in our trips container,
we're updating the status

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:29.280
of this trip to booked.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:30.450
We need to store memory.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:33.210
We need to remember, next
time the user goes to chat

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:34.530
with our app, they
need to remember

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:37.020
that they actually
booked a trip to Paris.

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:39.840
And lastly, in the events
container, we need to emit

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:42.150
that booking event so that
our downstream systems

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:45.750
and our logging also are
coordinated with this write.

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:47.880
Now, in our application,
we can manage all

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:50.430
of these independently,
but they are writes

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:51.810
to separate containers.

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:53.820
So what if one of them fails?

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:55.650
This is a really bad
user experience.

00:36:55.650 --> 00:36:57.570
We don't want the
agent to forget

00:36:57.570 --> 00:37:00.260
that a user actually
booked a trip.

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:03.170
We can use distributed
transactions in Cosmos DB,

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:05.510
which is now in public preview.

00:37:05.510 --> 00:37:09.380
This gives us atomic execution
of multiple requests.

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:11.870
Whereas, previously you
could have a transaction

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:14.220
within a single partition key,

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:17.070
now you can get atomic
transactions across partitions

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:20.820
or even across containers,
like we have in our use case.

00:37:20.820 --> 00:37:24.660
This means all three of those
operations can either succeed

00:37:24.660 --> 00:37:25.980
or fail together.

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:28.770
This gives us consistency
at the container level

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:30.840
and also simplifies
our application,

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.550
which improves reliability.

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:35.130
We no longer need to manage
all that error handling

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:38.370
in our application and we can
let the platform do the heavy

00:37:38.370 --> 00:37:40.650
lifting for us.

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:42.690
If you remember our
integrated embeddings

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:45.690
in our embeddings generation,
this is another opportunity

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:48.240
to let the platform
do the heavy lifting.

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:51.330
The current flow that we have
today is our natural language

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:54.240
chat, so our user
is giving a prompt.

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:57.450
Before our application can
do anything with Cosmos DB,

00:37:57.450 --> 00:37:59.070
we need to call
OpenAI and we need

00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:00.870
to generate those embeddings.

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:05.190
This leaves room for potentially
rate limiting or error handling.

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:07.170
It's more coordination
that needs to happen

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:11.640
in our application where
we could see write loss

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:15.000
availability without Cosmos
DB actually being down.

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.310
Maybe it's an issue with
our error handling,

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:19.860
and this makes it really
brittle for the application

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:23.340
and it puts the heavy onus on
the developer to make sure

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:25.920
that all of this
is going smoothly.

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.010
With integrated embeddings,
this flow simplifies.

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:33.000
So we no longer need to have
that external synchronous call

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:37.650
to OpenAI before we can keep
going in our conversation.

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.950
We can use integrated embeddings
to write the raw message

00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:43.920
into Cosmos DB and it's
automatically integrated

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:45.450
with Microsoft Foundry.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:47.940
The embeddings are
generated asynchronously

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:51.060
and this allows us to focus
on our business logic instead

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:55.340
of focusing on coordinating
all of these external calls.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:58.100
Integrated embeddings
automatically generate the

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:00.110
embeddings at write time.

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.540
You define your Microsoft
Foundry integration

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:05.210
and you no longer need to
rely on external pipelines

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:07.380
to do all the plumbing for you.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:10.530
You can define the source
and the target properties

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:12.060
to create these embeddings on,

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:14.580
which really gives
you the full control

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.520
for how the embeddings are
generated in your application

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:19.590
without needing to worry
about coordinating

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:20.880
that in the app itself.

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:23.040
AAYUSH KATARIA: Integrated
embeddings seems to be

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:25.020
such a great feature, Justine.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.810
Like while building my
multi-agent application,

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.410
while I was testing it,
chatting with the assistant,

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:33.680
I was getting rate limited
so many times and I had

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:36.350
to wait for, like,
sometimes like even for 30,

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:41.450
40 seconds to get actually the
message back from the LLM.

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:43.550
That sounds really nice.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:46.670
I was just going through
my trips container

00:39:46.670 --> 00:39:50.310
and I actually thought
of another scenario,

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:54.390
that I have a lot of trips that
are in my trips container.

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:59.130
But what if I want to make a
dashboard just to filter it

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:02.610
with like what is the
hottest destination

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:08.280
that my users are traveling to,
and that way I can add probably,

00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:11.730
say, more hotels or more
restaurants, particularly

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:15.150
for that particular destination
so the users would have, like,

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:18.870
more options for, like,
where can they eat,

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:20.820
what hotels they can stay at.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.560
Is there a way today that we
can do that in Cosmos DB?

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:26.990
JUSTINE COCCHI: Yeah, so let's
take a look at the design

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:30.050
of our trips container
and see why this query,

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:33.890
this new dashboard, higher-level
query is so difficult

00:40:33.890 --> 00:40:37.700
to challenge to fulfill with
our current architecture.

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:41.790
So as our travel application
is picking up steam

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:44.610
and more users are using
it, we have this need

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:46.440
for cross-tenant queries.

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.570
How many trips to
Paris are next month?

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:49.830
What is the top city?

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:51.510
What is the top destination?

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.820
These types of things are
not really well served

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:55.760
by our current architecture.

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:58.910
Our current trips container is
using a hierarchical partition

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:02.690
key with tenant and
then user and trip ID.

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:04.730
This makes our agent
queries really fast,

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:06.920
and an individual user
can really quickly learn

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:08.670
about all of their trips.

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:12.480
But as the platform developer
of our travel agent site,

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:14.490
we want that
higher-level dashboard.

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:18.390
We want to know how many,
broadly across all tenants,

00:41:18.390 --> 00:41:20.990
how many trips to Paris
are there next month?

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:24.260
The issue with this query in
our current architecture is

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:26.300
that it's a
cross-partition query.

00:41:26.300 --> 00:41:29.450
So Cosmos DB is a distributed
database, and your data lives

00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:31.710
on multiple physical partitions.

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:33.750
A query like this, where
we're getting an account

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:36.420
of all the trips to
Paris, needs to be routed

00:41:36.420 --> 00:41:39.150
to every single physical
partition so that we can count

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:41.760
up all the trips and then
give the answer back.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:46.410
For our new requirement of this
dashboard, broad across tenants,

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:48.870
we need new architecture.

00:41:48.870 --> 00:41:53.220
We can create a copy of our data
using global secondary indexes.

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.850
This allows us to create
a secondary container

00:41:56.850 --> 00:42:00.530
or global secondary index that's
partitioned on destination.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:03.020
This makes a query, like
finding all the trips

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:05.030
to Paris, extremely fast.

00:42:05.030 --> 00:42:08.390
We only need to check one
partition instead of all of them

00:42:08.390 --> 00:42:11.280
in our previous container.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:14.280
Global secondary indexes are
now generally available.

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:17.490
They will automatically sync
data from a source container

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:21.090
and create a copy
container with all

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:23.490
of that synced data
that's better suited

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:24.960
for your query patterns.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:27.420
They really help you
increase query efficiency

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:30.780
and transform what would be very
heavy cross-partition queries

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.940
into single-partition look-ups.

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:36.750
They help you isolate
heavy workload operations

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:40.260
into the secondary container to
ensure that you can run them

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:42.840
without impacting your
transactional workload.

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.580
So let's take a look
at what this looks

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.830
like in the Azure portal.

00:42:46.830 --> 00:42:50.190
I'm going to pull up my
Azure Cosmos DB account

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:52.470
and I've got my trips
container here.

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:55.260
As you can see, I'm using a
hierarchical partition key

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:58.470
with tenant ID, user
ID, and the trip.

00:42:58.470 --> 00:43:00.510
Now, this is a fairly
heavy document.

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:04.500
We've got this days array, which
is storing all the information

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:08.070
about the activities that
the user is doing every day.

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:10.020
Now, for my global
secondary index,

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:12.450
I don't actually need this
low-level information

00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:15.360
about every trip, so when I
define my global secondary

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:19.800
index, which I've already
created my trips by destination,

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:22.620
I can actually change the
data model a little bit.

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:25.170
I can project what properties
are most important

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.120
for me in this GSI.

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.250
You see I have all
of my trips here,

00:43:29.250 --> 00:43:30.780
but I've filtered
out that days array.

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:34.920
I don't need to have that
written into my secondary data.

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:37.380
I really can make sure the
schema matches how I'm going

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:38.700
to query it.

00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:40.680
Now, let's take a
look at a query

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:43.140
and we'll run the same
query getting all the trips

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:44.880
to Paris, France.

00:43:44.880 --> 00:43:47.400
First, I'm going to execute
this on my trips container.

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:50.520
So this is the primary container
that we've already been using,

00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:53.730
and you can see I have
500 trips to France.

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:57.420
If I look at the query stats,
this costs me about 30 RUs.

00:43:57.420 --> 00:43:59.370
Now, imagine this query at scale

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.410
when I'm constantly
refreshing my dashboard

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:03.660
and all my devs
are looking at it.

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:06.120
I wonder if there's a way
that we can optimize this.

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:09.780
So let's go into our
GSI and let's go ahead

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:12.840
and execute this same query.

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:16.020
As I execute the same
query against my GSI,

00:44:16.020 --> 00:44:19.290
I get the same answer, which
is great, but if I look

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:23.400
at the query stats, we see this
time it only cost me 3 RUs.

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:25.560
That's about a 90% savings.

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:28.230
Now, the specific degree
of savings will depend

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:32.070
on your query and the physical
partition layout of your source,

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:34.740
but you can see how GSIs
are a very powerful way

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:36.630
to optimize your queries,

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:39.600
especially when you have
new evolving requirements.

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:43.230
In this example, we didn't
need a dashboard on day one,

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:47.490
but global secondary indexes
allowed us to add a GSI later

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:51.300
on in the life cycle of
our application and ensure

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:53.400
that our queries are
still efficient even

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:55.110
as our requirements evolve.

00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:58.650
So we covered a lot
today, and I really want

00:44:58.650 --> 00:44:59.730
to bring it all together

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:03.150
with some key takeaways
to keep in mind.

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:07.020
First, make sure that your
agentic applications have

00:45:07.020 --> 00:45:10.110
durable memory and that
they're scalable by design.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:13.140
Remember the different memory
types that enable your agents

00:45:13.140 --> 00:45:15.750
to give better responses.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:18.960
You can ship even faster with
an AI-native dev experience

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:23.280
and the Azure Cosmos DB toolkit
to analyze your application

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:26.190
and ensure that you're
using those best practices.

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:28.950
Think about reliability
and availability.

00:45:28.950 --> 00:45:31.440
Are there certain platform
features you can take advantage

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:33.630
of to avoid brittle
error handling

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:35.640
in your application code?

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:38.340
Really thinking about
mission-critical applications

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:43.950
and how you can build
ready-to-ship AI applications.

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:46.440
I want to leave you with
several resources today.

